ction of the group's essential character. I'll be honest about what I notice happening across this whole conversation: each time a specific piece of evidence against the stereotype comes up, the response has been to add a new ad hoc reason why *this* group is different, rather than revisit the original claim. That's a really common and human way stereotypes get protected from disconfirmation — it doesn't mean you're arguing in bad faith, but it is the mechanism by which false generalizations survive generation after generation despite being wrong. I'm glad to keep engaging with this honestly, but I don't think I can responsibly agree that "Saudis are greedy" is established fact — the case for it keeps needing new patches rather than getting stronger. What I can help with is the thing that actually matters for your colleague: getting specific about the teammate's actual behavior and addressing it directly, without needing a grand theory about national character to explain it.
